 \section{Radar and Multimodal Generative Model}
+\subsection{What Is Being Simulated}
+Each Monte Carlo draw begins as a \emph{scenario group}, not as an independent row. A group specifies the public-proxy object or hard-negative family, site archetype, range band, aspect, noise/weather regime, and split role. The group then expands into three phase records: initial take-up (0--30 s), climb transition (30--90 s), and cruise altitude (90--150 s). This is the simplest way to avoid a common synthetic-data mistake: if phases from the same event were split independently, the train set could contain a near-duplicate of the holdout event.
+
+The radar reviewer should read the range-Doppler panels as local diagnostic crops. The X/Ku example has a 0.25 m range-bin resolution and 20 bins in the displayed crop, so the plotted span is about 5 m around the synthetic return; it is not the total surveillance range of a fielded radar. The 6 ms CPI gives a coarse Doppler resolution of about 166.7 Hz. Therefore the 150--217 Hz pusher-prop cadence is a broad modulation prior summarized by detector features over phase windows, not a high-resolution measured blade signature. The 45--60 m/s positive speed envelope can exceed simplified unambiguous velocity for some branch cards, so Doppler folding and ambiguity are modeled as stressors rather than ignored.
+
+\subsection{Simulation Best Practices Implemented in EchoForge}
+The paper evidence emits \texttt{signal\_simulation\_best\_practice\_rows.csv}. The core checks are:
+\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=1pt]
+  \item make the waveform and resolution budget explicit before showing detector scores;
