+\section{Detector-View Processing Best Practices}
+Once synthetic radar/cue products exist, EchoForge deliberately narrows them into detector-view products. That boundary is a feature, not a limitation: it lets a reviewer ask exactly what a classical branch, ML branch, fusion branch, or EI candidate could see. The appendix crosswalk in Fig.~\ref{fig:detectorlookup} maps detector families to code listings and reference families. Classical radar branches expose CFAR/MTD-style summaries, micro-Doppler spread, range-Doppler concentration, clutter stress, and track confidence. ML controls see detector-facing numerical summaries only. Fusion sees branch confidence, missingness, stale-track, and source-quality features. Labels, split keys, group IDs, time locks, and generator internals are audit fields and remain denied to model-facing matrices.
