+\section{Sensor Fusion as a First-Class Baseline}
+Sensor fusion deserves its own section because the accepted comparator is not a weak radar-only straw baseline. EchoForge models a late-fusion setting where each phase record carries branch scores and source-quality terms: X/Ku, S-band, GBAD cueing, acoustic cadence/agreement, passive-RF no-signal/RFI/provenance geometry, stale-track state, and missingness. The accepted human-engineered prior fusion baseline uses these detector-view products with train/CV thresholding. EI is compared against that accepted-practice fusion baseline, not merely against individual radar branches.
+
+The fusion design follows four rules. First, every branch score must be traceable to an allowed detector-view feature family. Second, absence of a cue is represented explicitly; RF silence, RFI contamination, and stale tracks are not silently imputed as positive evidence. Third, calibration is monotone and fitted without holdout rows. Fourth, the paper reports when a view control looks suspiciously strong. In this run the passive-RF-only control is stronger than the full EI candidate on AP and swept Recall@$\leq$1\%FPR but worse on selected-threshold F1 and ECE; this remains a required red-flag discussion and motivates passive-RF shuffle/missingness stress tests before any broader claim.

